{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Titan WACC Build and Valuation Sensitivity Analysis",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst on the Mumbai desk at a boutique investment firm. Your senior has asked you to build Titan's WACC from first principles using real Indian market inputs, then stress-test how sensitive the valuation is to small shifts in the discount rate. The firm's valuation model hinges on this one number — get it slightly wrong and the price target swings 20% without a single operating assumption changing.",
  "instructions": "Compute Titan's cost of equity using CAPM with Indian inputs, calculate the post-tax cost of debt, weight them by market value, and derive WACC. Then use WACC to discount a perpetuity and measure valuation sensitivity to a ±0.5% WACC shift.",
  "learning_objective": "Calculate Titan's WACC using CAPM, apply the tax shield to debt, weight by market value, and quantify how WACC movements swing valuation — exposing the leverage an analyst has to manufacture conclusions.",
  "hints": [
    "Remember: Cost of Equity = Risk-free rate + (Beta × Equity Risk Premium). Use Indian 10-Yr G-Sec ~7%, Titan beta ~0.82, India ERP ~6.5%.",
    "Post-tax Cost of Debt = Pre-tax rate × (1 − tax rate). The tax rate at Titan is ~25.17%, so a 9% borrowing cost becomes ~6.73% after the deduction benefit.",
    "WACC = (E/V) × Ke + (D/V) × Post-tax Kd, where V = market cap + net debt. Use the rounded figures provided; weight equity at 98.3% and debt at 1.7% of total value."
  ],
